    <h2 id="section-1" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">1. Statutory Framework Under Section 54 of Transfer of Property Act 1882</h2>
    <p>Section 54 of the Transfer of Property Act defines "Sale" as a transfer of ownership in exchange for a price paid, promised, or part-paid and part-promised. Crucially, the statute establishes a clear legal line between an executed sale and a contract for sale:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Sale Deed (Conveyance):</strong> A sale of tangible immovable property of value one hundred rupees and upwards can be made only by a registered instrument. It effects an immediate transfer of ownership, vesting absolute title in the purchaser.</li>
        <li><strong>Agreement to Sell (Contract for Sale):</strong> A contract for the sale of immovable property is a contract that a sale of such property shall take place on terms settled between the parties. Section 54 explicitly clarifies that an Agreement for Sale <em>"does not, of itself, create any interest in or charge on such property."</em></li>
    </ul>

