    <h2 id="section-3" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">3. Landmark Supreme Court Rulings Governing Title Conveyance</h2>
    <p>The legal enforceability of sales agreements vs sale deeds has been clarified through several landmark judgments by the Supreme Court of India:</p>
    <h3>A. Suraj Lamp & Industries Pvt. Ltd. vs. State of Haryana (2012)</h3>
    <p>In this historic ruling, the Supreme Court held that property transfers executed via General Power of Attorney (GPA), Sale Agreements, and Wills (collectively termed SA/GPA/WILL transactions) do not convey title or create ownership interest in immovable property. The court held:</p>
    <blockquote style="background: rgba(241, 245, 249, 0.8); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); padding: 15px; margin: 15px 0;">
        <em>"Immovable property can be legally transferred only by a registered deed of conveyance. An agreement to sell, with or without delivery of possession, is not a deed of conveyance."</em>
    </blockquote>
    <h3>B. Rambhau Namdeo Gajre vs. Narayan Bapuji Dhotra (2004)</h3>
    <p>The apex court confirmed that the protection of <strong>Part Performance under Section 53A</strong> of the Transfer of Property Act is available only as a shield to protect possession, not as a sword to claim legal title or ownership without a registered Sale Deed.</p>

    <h2 id="section-4" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">4. Role of RERA in Agreement for Sale Mandatory Standardisation</h2>
    <p>Under the <strong>Real Estate (Regulation and Development) Act, 2016 (RERA)</strong>, Section 13 mandates that real estate promoters cannot accept a sum exceeding 10% of the apartment or plot cost as an advance deposit without entering into a written, registered <strong>Agreement for Sale</strong> with the allottee.</p>
    <p>Unlike traditional agreements, RERA-mandated Agreements for Sale include statutory clauses regarding construction timelines, penalty interest for delay, sanctioned architectural plans, carpet area definitions, and mandatory defect liability coverage for 5 years post-handover.</p>

    <h2 id="section-5" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">5. Essential Clauses Required in a Water-Tight Sale Deed</h2>
    <p>When transitioning from an Agreement to Sell to a final Sale Deed at the Sub-Registrar Office (SRO), ensure the following legal covenants are incorporated:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Description of Parties:</strong> Complete names, residential addresses, PAN numbers, and Aadhaar verification details of vendor and vendee.</li>
        <li><strong>Title Traceability & Recitals:</strong> Chronological recital of past title deeds detailing how the seller acquired ownership (Sale, Gift, Inheritance, Partition).</li>
        <li><strong>Full Consideration & Payment Schedule:</strong> Specific breakdown of advance earnest money, cheque/NEFT/RTGS transaction IDs, and balance consideration paid.</li>
        <li><strong>Absolute Transfer Clause:</strong> Express legal language confirming the seller relinquishes all right, title, interest, and claim in favor of the purchaser forever.</li>
        <li><strong>Encumbrance-Free Indemnity Covenant:</strong> A explicit warranty from the seller declaring the property free from all mortgages, bank liens, tax arrears, court attachments, and family disputes, pledging to indemnify the buyer against future title losses.</li>
        <li><strong>Schedule of Property:</strong> Detailed physical description including plot dimensions, survey numbers, door numbers, ward numbers, and exact cardinal boundaries (North, South, East, West).</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="section-6" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">6. SRO Registration Procedure and Stamp Duty Calculation</h2>
    <p>To register a Sale Deed under Section 17 of the Registration Act, follow this step-by-step procedure:</p>
    <ol class="step-list">
        <li><strong>Calculate Stamp Duty & Registration Fees:</strong> Check the jurisdictional SRO Guideline Value / Circle Rate. Stamp duty is calculated on the higher value between the agreement consideration and the SRO guideline value.</li>
        <li><strong>Generate E-Stamp Paper:</strong> Purchase electronic stamp paper (e-Stamp) through designated state treasury portals (SHCIL, TNREGINET, Kaveri 2.0, StockHolding).</li>
        <li><strong>Draft Deed Text:</strong> Prepare the final Sale Deed text on stamp paper, leaving mandatory margins for SRO biometric endorsement stamps.</li>
        <li><strong>Book SRO Slot:</strong> Schedule an appointment on the official state SRO portal for document presentation.</li>
        <li><strong>Biometric Attendance & Photo Verification:</strong> Both buyer and seller, along with two independent witnesses, must present original government photo IDs (Aadhaar, PAN) at the SRO for thumbprint and photo capture.</li>
        <li><strong>Issuance of Registered Document:</strong> SRO assigns a unique Book 1 Registration Number (Volume and Page Index II entry) and hands over the certified registered Sale Deed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="section-7" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">7. Encumbrance Certificate (EC) Verification Post Sale Deed</h2>
    <p>After completing SRO registration, buyers must conduct a post-registration Encumbrance Certificate search (Form 15) after 7 to 14 days. The newly registered Sale Deed document number, seller name, buyer name, and property extent must appear on the official state EC portal (TNREGINET, Kaveri 2.0, IGRS AP, Dharani TS). This verifies that the SRO index has officially updated the property ownership ledger.</p>

    <h2 id="section-8" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">8. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
    <div class="faq-container">
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4 class="faq-question">Q1: What is the primary difference between a Sale Deed and an Agreement to Sell?</h4>
            <p class="faq-answer">A Sale Deed immediately transfers complete legal ownership and title of property from seller to buyer upon execution and SRO registration. An Agreement to Sell is an executory contract promising to transfer ownership at a future date subject to agreed terms and conditions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4 class="faq-question">Q2: Does an Agreement to Sell create ownership rights in property?</h4>
            <p class="faq-answer">No. Under Section 54 of the Transfer of Property Act 1882, an Agreement to Sell does not by itself create any interest or charge on property. Full legal title passes only upon execution and SRO registration of a final Sale Deed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4 class="faq-question">Q3: Can a home loan be sanctioned based only on an Agreement to Sell?</h4>
            <p class="faq-answer">Banks issue initial loan approval letters based on a registered Agreement for Sale, but final loan disbursement occurs only after the Sale Deed is executed, registered at the SRO, and pledged to the bank as a registered mortgage (MODTD).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4 class="faq-question">Q4: What happens if the seller refuses to execute the Sale Deed after taking earnest money?</h4>
            <p class="faq-answer">The buyer can file a Suit for Specific Performance under the Specific Relief Act, 1963 in civil court, asking the court to order the seller to execute the Sale Deed or execute it on their behalf.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4 class="faq-question">Q5: Is stamp duty paid on an Agreement for Sale adjustable against the Sale Deed stamp duty?</h4>
            <p class="faq-answer">Yes, in most states (e.g., Maharashtra, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu), stamp duty paid on a registered Agreement for Sale is set off against the total stamp duty payable during final Sale Deed registration.</p>
